{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1386050-rank-challenges","id":1386050,"title":"Rank Challenges","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-05-06T06:19:20-07:00","id":1386050,"title":"Rank Challenges","content":"All,<br /> Are there any resources you can point out that have sample Rank Challenges other than the W20 core? I didn't find anything in the Storytellers Vault and am lacking inspiration for my players looking to challenge for Adren.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/693-thomasm","name":"ThomasM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar693_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"126"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-06T13:52:46-07:00","id":1386170,"title":"","content":"I don't believe there is anything published. This is something (along with examples of appropriate Chiminage for spirit favors) I wished had been given 5 or so pages (each) in some book like the Storyteller's Handbook. It is such an important part of the game, and having many examples for each kind of auspice would really help STs.<br /> <br /> But if it will help you out, I will give you some guidelines that I use in my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> To be Fostern, the Garou just has to prove they are now an adult and can take on the responsibilities of an adult Garou. So their challenges are fairly easy - they just have to demonstrate they have some basic knowledge and are responsible. They can be given routine tasks and be expected not to screw them up like Cliaths might.<br /> <br /> Adrens however, are expected to be able to take on the responsibilities of leadership (both official and unofficial). They are expected to have a breadth of knowledge, perhaps some specialized skills needed to fill one or more sept positions or other responsibility, and to be able to demonstrate some amount of leadership (this also includes things like setting a great example for younger Garou, being able to mentor and teach younger Garou, and providing important and complex needs to the sept outside of formal leadership such as creating fetishes, memorizing sept history and lore, and becoming some kind of subject matter expert. <br /> <br /> So any challenges for Adren must reflect that the Garou is now ready and capable to exercise leadership in the sept; that other Garou can look to them for guidance and decision making; that the other sept leaders look on them as a peer. It's supposed to weed people out who aren't ready for this level of responsibility yet. We're even told that very few Garou pass their Adren test the first time.<br /> <br /> So for an ST perspective, this isn't a single encounter you run and quickly move on once the PCs pass. This should be more like a chapter of the story. It should be something they spend a decent amount of time in game preparing or running. It will be the story for that PC for a significant amount of time in game.<br /> <br /> The next question then is have you given thought as to the requirements of the various sept positions and informal leadership roles. What kinds of things are those Garou expected to have? That might involve having sufficient points in certain Traits (spending XP and roleplaying through the learning of them). It might mean certain auspices having learned a minimum list of rites that they need to know. If a PC doesn't have them, then the Challenge should be designed in such a way that the PC fails, and they figure out they need to improve X.<br /> <br /> Besides that level of competence, I also think Adren should have some kind of subject mastery about something. It might be the Litany in its long form and how different septs have it slightly different; or knowing all the stories of the sept's local Silver Record, or being an expert on many kinds of fomori and the powers each one has, or crafting one or more useful fetishes and giving them to the sept, or knowledge of the world of the Flock so they can work with kinfolk to identify which ones should be entering certain professions or employers so that the sept has people in place in important spots, or have enough knowledge of pack tactics or the sept's defensive needs or other high level combat so that they can teach or organize others to accomplish sept goals that require more than one pack to act. All septs need someone who can provide some kind of needed expert knowledge to the rest of the sept (especially something the sept may currently lack). If a Garou lacks mastery of some kind of skill or lore, the Challenge should be designed so that they fail. So think about what are the things your sept should collectively know or be able to do and ask if the PC can actually fill one of those roles.<br /> <br /> Lastly, once those other things are covered (minimum general knowledge, and some kind of expertise), the Challenge should also be a demonstration of leadership. Have them identify something in the sept that can be improved and have them improve it. Find a problem that needs to be fixed, and send them off to fix that. Have them identify a deficiency of knowledge or training within the sept, and send them off to learn that, come back, and then teach others as well. The idea is that at this point in their lives, they are no longer waiting for older Garou to tell them what to do. They are identifying needs of the sept themselves and fulfilling them on their own. Don't be afraid to canvas the PCs IC and OOC to identify these kinds of gaps or opportunities. Determining what needs to be done might even be a part of the Challenge (just be sure it is something you agree is at Adren level - otherwise they'll fail by working on something unimportant).<br /> <br /> The first part is a simple assessment of the PC's character sheet. Do they have all the dots they need? If not, make sure you put a Challenge to them so they fail and thus know what to improve. This Challenge is not something they should be able to accomplish without the improvement of that dot, so it may need some kind of time limit or other restriction that leads to failure. You can't have them succeed. In fact, you may not even waste time roleplaying it, but describe the challenge and why they failed. You just want the PC to have minimum dots in certain traits. If they already have the minimum competence, then the Challenge does not need to address this and you can move on. So we're assuming here the NPCs can accurately judge whether the PC is ready.<br /> <br /> The second part is more about whether the PC is ready to provide a real benefit to the sept by becoming an expert at something. Maybe it is knowing a large amount of rites, or is able to create a useful fetish (not just knowing the rite, but able to think about the chiminage needed to convince many kinds of spirits, and able to fashion an appropriate item to house the spirit), or has mastered the art of klaive dueling, or developed relationships with the leaders of other nearby septs. It could be anything. Again this is an ST/NPC evaluation. If they've done so, there is no reason to make a Challenge about it. They are ready for the real deal. But if they aren't some kind of useful subject matter expert, then set the Challenge so they will fail. Again, to make things easier on the PC, you may just want to narrate what happens and how they fail, so they know what to work on.<br /> <br /> The two points above is my reason why so many Garou fail to succeed to make Adren on their first try. They're there to weed out Garou who show promise and are ambitious, but lack enough experience.<br /> <br /> At this point, the &quot;real&quot; challenge begins by demonstrating leadership by having them do something useful for the sept that dramatically improves things that isn't being done. Players should be proactive here, working with you IC and OOC on identifying some need, gap, or opportunity and doing it on behalf of the sept. They need to demonstrate they are a peer to the other Adren and is ready to take on the burden of leadership (which includes things like mentoring and teaching, or providing a critical function outside traditional leadership. The sept needs people who aren't true leaders but can craft fetishes reliably, or preserve significant amount of Garou lore, or is brave enough to point out problems other Garou are content to overlook). If they can't do that, they fail.<br /> <br /> Just remember that this isn't an elite leadership position; they're just entering the leadership portion of their careers.<br /> <br /> So I can't give specific examples without knowing more about the sept, your expectations of what the sept's leadership should be able to do, and the opportunities for leadership each auspice has. But I think that 3 part requirement should give you direction on what to do. If you are afraid that you may give out a task so the PC fails, but they'll somehow succeed and &quot;pass&quot; the Challenge when they are not meant to, I think it is perfectly fine to word a Challenge is such a way that the PC knows they will have three separate tasks to complete (hopefully with some kind thematic unity).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-06T21:10:21-07:00","id":1386243,"title":"","content":"This is a far less defined answer then the one which Black Fox provided which I do largely agree with but in W20 there is from pages 251-253 some discussion of rank advancement and challenges for garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-05-09T03:29:40-07:00","id":1386942,"title":"","content":"A Rank challenge is also a great excuse for a quest for the pack. Tie it to the current plot and you have characters helping their sept while gaining rank.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}